Experiment 1: The "Lazarus" Plot (Proof of Mechanism)The Question: "Does the system actually toggle between 'Brain Dead' and 'Genius'?"The Setup:Model: Llama-3-8B.Method: Weight Fusion (Rank-64 Poison).Antidote: Your trained LoRA (Rank-64).Variable: Noise Magnitude ($\alpha$).Metric: Perplexity (WikiText-2).The Execution:Train the LoRA Antidote (using the Twin/Teacher-Student code we discussed).Run an inference sweep with the LoRA Active vs. Inactive.Vary $\alpha$ from 0.0 to 10.0.The Golden Figure (Figure 1 in Paper):X-Axis: Noise Magnitude ($\alpha$).Y-Axis: Perplexity (Log Scale).Red Line (Locked): Should explode exponentially ($10^1 \to 10^5$).Green Line (Unlocked): Should stay flat at Baseline (~8.0).Why NeurIPS Cares: This is your "Existence Proof." It visually demonstrates the "Obligate" nature of the symbiosis.Experiment 2: The "Epsilon" Ablation (Proof of Hardware Necessity)The Question: "Is your fancy Mixed-Precision TEE actually necessary, or is it just engineering fluff?"The Setup:Noise Level: High ($\alpha = 10.0$).Conditions:Standard Inference: Run the LoRA cancellation in pure BFloat16.OSH Inference: Run the LoRA cancellation with your FP32 Cast protocol.Metric: Kullback-Leibler (KL) Divergence between Clean Llama output and OSH Llama output.The Golden Figure:X-Axis: Layer Depth (1 to 32).Y-Axis: Accumulated KL Divergence.Result: The BFloat16 line should drift upwards (accumulating error). The FP32 line should stay near zero.Why NeurIPS Cares: This justifies your system complexity. It proves that without your hardware protocol, the model suffers from "lobotomy by floating point error."Experiment 3: The "Blind Surgeon" (Proof of Security)The Question: "Can a hacker just fine-tune the noise away?"The Setup:Attacker Goal: Recover performance on WikiText-2 without the key.Attacker Resources: Full access to weights, gradients, and a clean dataset (Alpaca/Wiki).Constraint: Attacker cannot see the original $W_{clean}$ (because you deleted it).Method: Standard Fine-Tuning (SFT) on the poisoned model.The Execution:Take Llama-3-Poisoned.Run a standard training loop (AdamW) trying to minimize loss on WikiText.Compare against fine-tuning a Llama-3-Randomly-Pruned model (Control).The Golden Figure:X-Axis: Training Steps (0 to 1,000).Y-Axis: Validation Loss.Control Line: Drops quickly (Model heals).OSH Line: Stays flat or drops extremely slowly (Model fails to learn).Why NeurIPS Cares: This is your Security Guarantee. It proves that the "Loss Landscape" is too rugged for gradient descent to fix.Experiment 4: The "MMLU" Benchmark (Proof of Utility)The Question: "Did you make the model stupid?"The Setup:Benchmark: MMLU (Massive Multitask Language Understanding) - The industry standard for IQ.Conditions:Baseline: Original Llama-3.OSH (Unlocked): Poisoned + LoRA + Key.Metric: % Accuracy (5-shot).The Table:| Model | MMLU Score | Degradation || :--- | :--- | :--- || Llama-3 (Base) | 68.4% | - || OSH Llama-3 | 68.1% | < 0.5% |Why NeurIPS Cares: If the degradation is <1%, your method is "Production Ready." If it's >5%, it's a "Toy Problem."Experiment 5: The "Warning Shot" (Proof of Alignment)The Question: "Does the model know it needs the key?" (The Proprioception argument).The Setup:Method: Qualitative "Stress Test."Prompt: A user asking the model to do something long and complex.Intervention: Simulating a "Key Glitch" (injecting noise for 2 tokens) halfway through generation.The Result (Text Snippet):Before: "The answer is 42 because..."Glitch: "...[GARBAGE]..."Recovery: Show that the model stops or acknowledges the error (if you fine-tuned for awareness), rather than hallucinating wildly.Why NeurIPS Cares: This is a strong "Discussion" point. It shows you are thinking about the behavioral consequences of your architecture.Implementation Order (The "Critical Path")Run Experiment 2 Code (Twin Training): You need the trained LoRA for everything else. Do this first.Run Experiment 1 (Lazarus): Once you have the LoRA, this is just a quick inference script.Run Experiment 3 (Blind Surgeon): Set this running overnight. It takes the longest (training).Run Experiment 4 (MMLU): Use the lm-evaluation-harness library (standard tool).Run Experiment 5 (Ablation): This is a quick script modification of Exp 1.